# AMD Module Rules

Production code in `KDB3/js/**` ships as AMD modules. Cognos loads our production code through RequireJS; the host does not understand ES module `import` / `export`. This rule fixes the module shape and forbids forms that would silently fail in the host.

## Define Shape

Every production file consists of exactly one top-level `define(...)` call:

```js
define([
  "module/path/createKdbControlController.js",
  "module/path/createHistoryService.js"
], function (_createKdbControlController, _createHistoryService) {
  function __orchestrator(_KDB, _options) {
    const controller = _createKdbControlController({ /* deps */ });
    const historyService = _createHistoryService({ /* deps */ });
    return controller;
  }

  return __orchestrator;
});
```

The factory function returns exactly one value: the orchestrator function, the controller factory, the service factory, an object literal, or a single helper function. A `define` factory that returns multiple unrelated exports is a code-smell and must be split.

AMD import bindings carry the underscore prefix for boundary inputs per `naming.mdc`. The dependency array order matches the parameter order one-to-one.

## Full-Compact Form

The factory body is compact: it wires named helpers and returns. Inline or nested subfunction definitions inside the returned entry function, controller body, callbacks or executable flow are forbidden. **Named helper functions and the named entry function itself may live at AMD factory scope below or above the entry function, or in separate files.** Factory scope is everything directly inside the `function (...) { ... }` passed to `define`, but outside the entry function's body.

### Allowed: named functions at AMD factory scope

```js
// Allowed: the entry function (__controller) and a helper (buildRowView)
// are BOTH named function declarations at AMD factory scope. Neither lives
// inside the other.
define(["./readWidgetData.js"], function (_readWidgetData) {
  function __controller(_KDB, _widgetId) {
    const rows = _readWidgetData(_KDB, _widgetId);
    return rows.map(row => buildRowView(row));
  }

  function buildRowView(row) {
    return { id: row.id, label: row.title };
  }

  return __controller;
});
```

A factory may contain multiple named helpers at factory scope. Naming them as `function declarations` (not `const fn = function () {}` and not `const fn = () => {}`) keeps hoisting predictable and signals "this is a module-scope helper, not local logic".

### Allowed: factory-scope async helper for fire-and-forget effect chains

A named method on a service or controller that starts an async effect (Cognos call, fetch, long-running coordination) must not inline an `async function () { ... }()` IIFE inside the method body. Lift the async work into a named factory-scope helper that takes `_KDB`, the method arguments and the `AbortSignal` as parameters:

```js
define([
  "./effects/loadTableFromCognos.js",
  "./state/writers/markTableLoading.js",
  "./state/writers/markTableLoaded.js",
  "./state/writers/markTableFailed.js",
  "./events/eventNames.js"
], function (_loadTableFromCognos, _markTableLoading, _markTableLoaded, _markTableFailed, _eventNames) {
  async function runReloadTableLoadWork(_KDB, tableName, signal) {
    try {
      const result = await _loadTableFromCognos(_KDB, { tableName, signal });
      _markTableLoaded(_KDB, { tableName, rows: result.rows, rowCount: result.rows.length });
      _KDB.events.emit(_eventNames.LOAD_FINISHED, { tableName, rowCount: result.rows.length });
      return result.rows;
    } catch (err) {
      _markTableFailed(_KDB, { tableName, errorCode: "loadFailed", errorMessage: String(err) });
      _KDB.events.emit(_eventNames.LOAD_FAILED, { tableName, errorCode: "loadFailed", errorMessage: String(err) });
      throw err;
    } finally {
      delete _KDB.runtime.tableData.pendingLoads[tableName];
      delete _KDB.runtime.tableData.listeners[tableName];
    }
  }

  function createTableDataService(_KDB) {
    function reloadTable(tableName) {
      _markTableLoading(_KDB, { tableName });
      const controller = new AbortController();
      _KDB.runtime.tableData.listeners[tableName] = controller;
      const p = runReloadTableLoadWork(_KDB, tableName, controller.signal);
      _KDB.runtime.tableData.pendingLoads[tableName] = p;
      return p;
    }
    return { reloadTable };
  }

  return createTableDataService;
});
```

The named method (`reloadTable`) stays a thin orchestration step: setup runtime entry, start the work, record the promise, return. The async helper (`runReloadTableLoadWork`) carries the `try / catch / finally` that closes over the work's state transitions and cleanup. Both live at factory scope; neither is nested inside the other.

### Forbidden: subfunction definitions inside the entry function or its callbacks

```js
// Forbidden: subfunction definition inside the factory's return path.
define(["./readWidgetData.js"], function (_readWidgetData) {
  return function (_KDB, _widgetId) {
    const buildRowView = function (row) { return { id: row.id, label: row.title }; };
    const rows = _readWidgetData(_KDB, _widgetId);
    return rows.map(row => buildRowView(row));
  };
});
```

```js
// Forbidden: subfunction definition inside a callback passed to a method on
// the entry function's return value or anywhere in the executable flow.
define(["./readWidgetData.js"], function (_readWidgetData) {
  function __controller(_KDB, _widgetId) {
    const rows = _readWidgetData(_KDB, _widgetId);
    return rows.map(function (row) {
      function buildRowView(r) { return { id: r.id, label: r.title }; }
      return buildRowView(row);
    });
  }
  return __controller;
});
```

Helpers extracted to module scope keep the entry function short, testable and easy to read for both humans and AI. The rule of thumb: **declare functions at factory scope, call them from inside the entry function**, never the other way around.

## Entry Function Names

Per `naming.mdc` and `feature-structure.mdc`:

| Role | Entry function name | Factory file name |
|---|---|---|
| Folder orchestrator | `__orchestrator` | `__orchestrator<FolderName>.js` |
| Normal executable file | `__controller` | `verb + noun.js` (e.g. `readWidgetData.js`) |
| Module controller factory | `createKdbControlController` | `createKdbControlController.js` |
| Service factory | `createTableDataService` | `createTableDataService.js` |
| Adapter factory (ring 1 only) | `createCognosHostService` | `createCognosHostService.js` |

A factory file's `define` returns the corresponding factory function. An orchestrator's `define` returns the `__orchestrator` function. A normal executable file's `define` returns its `__controller`.

## Dependency Injection

Dependencies enter a factory in two ways, used together:

- **AMD imports** for code dependencies (other factories, pure helpers, constants).
- **Options object** for runtime dependencies the orchestrator wires (`_KDB`, services, adapters, event bus).

```js
define([
  "./state/createInitialState.js",
  "./effects/applyKdbControlEffects.js"
], function (_createInitialState, _applyKdbControlEffects) {
  function createKdbControlController(_deps) {
    const { _KDB, eventBus, historyService, tableDataService } = _deps;
    // ...
  }

  return createKdbControlController;
});
```

Deep files do not reach for `window._KDB`, `window.top.getKDB()` or `_KDB.services.<x>` to discover dependencies. Dependencies are explicit per `_____________KDB3API/architecture/projectStructureRules.md`.

A file with more than 6 AMD imports is a smell; more than 10 requires restructuring per `feature-structure.mdc`. Thin orchestrators may approach the limit when they only coordinate clearly named steps.

## Forbidden Forms

ES module syntax does not appear anywhere in production code:

```js
// Forbidden in KDB3/js/**:
import { something } from "./foo.js";
export function bar() { /* ... */ }
export default function () { /* ... */ }
```

Other forbidden shapes:

- **Multiple `define` calls in one file.** One module per file.
- **CommonJS `require(...)` calls** mixed with AMD `define`.
- **`define` with the simplified CommonJS wrapper** (`define(function (require, exports, module) { ... })`); use the explicit dependency-array form.
- **Conditional or dynamic `define` calls** (`if (cond) define(...);`).
- **Side-effects at module top level** outside the `define` factory; all initialisation runs inside the factory or through an orchestrator entry call.
- **Module-level state on the factory closure** that survives multiple instantiations; per-instance state lives on the controller instance, per-session state lives in `_KDB.state` / `_KDB.runtime`.

## Harness Exception

Files outside `KDB3/js/**` — local harnesses, test scaffolding, build-time tooling — may use ES modules when the harness runs in a non-AMD environment (Node, Vite, esbuild, plain `<script type="module">`). The boundary is hard: nothing inside `KDB3/js/**` imports from or exports to a harness file using ES module syntax.

A harness that exercises a production module imports it through the same AMD path the host uses (RequireJS in the harness page) or through a thin AMD-aware shim. Direct ES `import` of a production AMD file is forbidden.

## When AI Must Ask

The AI must explicitly ask the user when at least one of these triggers applies:

- a planned factory file would return more than one unrelated export;
- a needed shape does not fit `__orchestrator`, `__controller`, `create<Module>Controller`, `create<Service>Service` or `create<Adapter>Adapter`;
- inline subfunctions inside the factory cannot easily be lifted to module scope;
- a file is approaching the 6/10 import-count threshold and a clean split is non-obvious;
- existing legacy code uses the simplified CommonJS wrapper or multiple `define` calls and the safe migration target is unclear;
- a harness needs to interact with a production module in a way that is not covered by the AMD-aware shim approach.

Do not ask when:

- the file follows the canonical `define([...], function (...) { ... })` shape and returns one value;
- the entry function name matches the canonical role suffix;
- the dependencies fit the AMD-imports + options-object pattern.

Ask-template: *"For `<X>` I propose `<AMD shape | factory split | harness boundary>` because `<reason>`. Acceptable?"*

## Anti-Goals

- No ES module `import` / `export` in `KDB3/js/**`.
- No multiple `define` calls in one file; one module per file.
- No simplified CommonJS-style `define` wrapper.
- No conditional or dynamic `define` calls.
- No side-effects at module top level outside the `define` factory.
- No inline or nested subfunction definitions inside the entry function, callbacks or return path; extract to named AMD-factory-scope helpers or separate files.
- No inline `async function () { ... }()` IIFE inside a named method body; lift the async work into a named factory-scope helper that takes `_KDB`, the method arguments and the `AbortSignal` as parameters.
- No `define` factory that returns multiple unrelated exports; split into separate files.
- No deep files reaching for `window._KDB`, `window.top.getKDB()` or other globals to discover dependencies; injection comes from the orchestrator via the options object.
- No mixed AMD + CommonJS `require(...)` in production code.
- No module-level state on the factory closure that survives multiple instantiations.
- No direct ES `import` of a production AMD file from a harness.

